do put it in the Q&A. Nothing a lot more than good debates.

00:19.519 --> 00:31.280
Agents.md, I've mentioned it several times. This, of course, is the file which you use to give to prep your LLM with some information

00:31.280 --> 00:38.320
it's particularly it's used by coding agents. And it's a markdown file.md is your markdown,

00:38.320 --> 00:43.600
which is a sort of shorthand form. It was it's sort of inspired by by thinking of a way that we

00:43.600 --> 00:49.159
could have something that could represent an HTML page, a web page with a sort of simpler,

00:49.159 --> 00:52.600
more human readable and writable version of it

00:52.600 --> 00:55.039
rather than all of the tags you see in HTML.

00:55.039 --> 00:57.719
So, the simple idea of this,

00:57.719 --> 00:59.119
if you're not familiar with Markdown,

00:59.119 --> 01:02.200
is that if you see like a hash, a single hash,

01:02.200 --> 01:04.040
like it says there with hash project summary,

01:04.040 --> 01:06.959
that means a big heading, an H1.

01:06.959 --> 01:10.439
Two hashes is like an H2, three hashes is like an H3.

01:10.439 --> 01:12.719
So it lets you have a sort of hierarchy of headings.

01:12.719 --> 01:15.159
And otherwise it's pretty simple, numbered lists,

01:15.159 --> 01:18.000
hyphens for bulleted lists, that kind of stuff.

01:18.000 --> 01:18.920
You pick it up fast.

01:18.920 --> 01:22.680
It's like a text file with some useful extra characters

01:22.680 --> 01:25.680
to show like a mark up, styling.

01:25.680 --> 01:28.560
And this is a format which LLM's love.

01:28.560 --> 01:31.640
They love markdown because they've been trained

01:31.640 --> 01:33.760
with lots and lots of Markdown. They've

01:33.760 --> 01:38.879
generated lots of Markdown, so they just love making Markdown files and they love reading them

01:38.879 --> 01:44.239
and they're easy to write as well. So it makes absolute sense that we would use these kinds of

01:44.239 --> 01:55.879
Markdown files to prep our agents. And you write them in natural language style, just like you might write a prompt to an LLM, which means you want it to be nice

01:55.879 --> 02:03.079
and concise and crisp and assertive and not have too much ambiguity and really have

02:03.079 --> 02:08.120
as much signal as you can in the words that you use. That's some of the tricks. This,

02:08.120 --> 02:13.960
of course, gets squashed into your context window, so this is precious space. So you

02:13.960 --> 02:17.280
don't want to be verbose. You want to make every word count.

02:17.280 --> 02:20.280
And so as I say, it's a markdown format file.

02:20.280 --> 02:23.800
It gets included in the context for the agent.

02:23.800 --> 02:28.319
And you typically have one of these files in what's called the project root directory,

02:28.319 --> 02:31.759
which is like for yesterday was the directory called instant.

02:31.759 --> 02:34.159
It's the directory at the top of your project.

02:34.159 --> 02:39.000
And that file will get read in and included in the context for the LLM.

02:39.000 --> 02:42.879
But you can also have another agents.md file

02:42.879 --> 02:46.280
in every subdirectory and at any level of nesting

02:46.280 --> 02:48.560
at a subsubsubdirectory.

02:48.560 --> 02:53.479
And in that case, that agents.md file will only be read in

02:53.479 --> 02:57.960
when the agent is working on files in that subsubdirectory

02:57.960 --> 03:00.560
or any further sub-directory.

03:00.560 --> 03:04.439
So it reads in when the agent is working on any file,

03:04.439 --> 03:08.840
it will read in the agents.md in that inner directory,

03:08.840 --> 03:10.199
and then it will work backwards

03:10.199 --> 03:11.400
through all the parent directories,

03:11.400 --> 03:15.560
making sure it's got all of the relevant agents.md files

03:15.560 --> 03:17.080
in context.

03:17.080 --> 03:19.139
And that's why I said that it's not true to say

03:19.139 --> 03:21.639
that agents.md are always included.

03:21.639 --> 03:24.240
They're always included if it's in the project route,

03:24.240 --> 03:27.240
but when it's in a sub-directory, it's only included

03:27.240 --> 03:31.000
if the agent is working on that subfolder.

03:31.000 --> 03:36.120
And typically, information that you put in the most inner agents.md

03:36.120 --> 03:39.520
overrides information in any outer directory.

03:39.520 --> 03:40.800
So you can be more specific.

03:40.800 --> 03:44.039
There's a new rule that should override the general rule.

03:44.039 --> 03:47.159
You put that deep down in a sub-directory.

03:47.159 --> 03:51.520
So that's how this sort of hierarchy of agents.md files works.

03:51.520 --> 03:52.500
And if that's not clear to you,

03:52.500 --> 03:55.639
don't worry because we'll be doing plenty of it in the next few weeks.

03:55.639 --> 04:00.159
And I say agents.md and that is in fact exactly the name of the file.

04:00.159 --> 04:08.379
If you're using cursor or codecs or GitHub co-pilot. Claude Code uses something called Claude.md

04:08.379 --> 04:12.780
and Antigravity uses something called Gemini.md.

04:12.780 --> 04:15.460
But it's the same idea, a markdown file

04:15.460 --> 04:17.740
that tells the agent what to do that you could have

04:17.740 --> 04:20.220
in the project route or in any subdirectory.

04:20.220 --> 04:24.019
And I should mention that cursor and GitHub co-pilot

04:24.019 --> 04:26.540
have other kinds of files as well,

04:26.540 --> 04:29.319
like cursor has these things called rules.

04:29.319 --> 04:32.079
But the trend that I see is that in time,

04:32.079 --> 04:34.879
everyone is converging on these agents.md.

04:34.879 --> 04:37.779
So while there are still people that use Kerse rules

04:37.779 --> 04:40.019
that generally speaking, more and more,

04:40.019 --> 04:43.160
people are just focusing on agents.md,

04:43.160 --> 04:45.959
that's become the kind of de facto standard

04:45.959 --> 04:49.000
agents.md or claw.md or gem.net.md.

04:49.000 --> 04:51.319
That's how people are doing things these days.

04:51.319 --> 04:53.519
But there are some other approaches too

04:53.519 --> 04:56.439
that some of the tools have specifically for those tools.

04:56.439 --> 04:58.720
So look, we're gonna spend a lot of time looking at

04:58.720 --> 05:00.920
what makes a good agent.md

05:00.920 --> 05:03.160
and what makes a bad agent.md

05:03.160 --> 05:05.439
and it's best to see that stuff in practice.

05:05.439 --> 05:08.680
But just to give you a few bit of a sense of it,

05:08.680 --> 05:10.120
you can see here the kinds of things

05:10.120 --> 05:13.439
that I tend to try and do in my ages.md. I

05:13.439 --> 05:20.639
obviously focus on giving it the overall project dolls and the success criteria. I like to give it

05:20.639 --> 05:25.839
a checklist that it can check things off on the success criteria so that it's really forced to go

05:25.839 --> 05:34.040
and do that. I also often list links to other documents that it can optionally load. We'll cover that in due course.

05:34.040 --> 05:37.639
And then I like to have the clear coding standards

05:37.639 --> 05:40.040
that apply to this project.

05:40.040 --> 05:43.000
And here it's so important to be concise,

05:43.000 --> 05:46.160
to be specific, and to try and correct

05:46.160 --> 05:49.600
for problems that you've had with the agent in the past.

05:49.600 --> 05:51.660
And of course, this is such an evolving space,

05:51.660 --> 05:53.319
and things that are a problem for me now

05:53.319 --> 05:55.399
may not be a problem anymore for you.

05:55.399 --> 05:58.000
But for example, I hate the way that

05:58.000 --> 06:02.399
these AI agents tend to over complicate everything. So I always try and stress,

06:02.399 --> 06:08.000
simpler is better, not too many comments, comments only when necessary. Be concise,

06:08.000 --> 06:13.360
have short readmes, I hate the way that LLM's love generating long, wordy readmes.

06:13.360 --> 06:20.819
I used to be so happy when people would send me PRs with code for me to merge into my open source repos.

06:20.819 --> 06:23.000
And then we'd have all of these lengthy readmees.

06:23.000 --> 06:25.160
The first time this happened a couple of years ago, I thought,

06:25.160 --> 06:28.079
"Wow, that's so kind of that person to write this long readme.

06:28.079 --> 06:29.500
And now I know better."

06:29.500 --> 06:32.699
These LLM generated readmees are just the worst.

06:32.699 --> 06:34.120
Don't do that, please.

06:34.120 --> 06:38.480
So I always tell LLM to make readmees short and no emojis.

06:38.480 --> 06:39.959
Please, no emojis.

06:39.959 --> 06:43.199
And then I say important. It turns out that it

06:43.199 --> 06:49.519
works well to put the word important in block capitals. Who knew? Important avoid over defensive

06:49.519 --> 06:55.839
programming, avoid is instance checks in Python, just horrible hacky stuff. Only manage exceptions

06:55.839 --> 07:04.079
when necessary. They loved to put tries around everything. And then for me, because I use UV the Python package manager, I might say

07:04.079 --> 07:11.120
use UV always UV run something rather than Python three something always never.

07:12.160 --> 07:16.240
And so you can see some different stylistic things there. You probably know this already,

07:16.240 --> 07:22.560
but use back ticks to wrap around code like that one back tick. If you have some some inline code,

07:22.560 --> 07:24.079
just a little bit of code, and you do

07:24.079 --> 07:28.720
three back ticks if you've got a whole block of code, three back ticks, code and another three

07:28.720 --> 07:32.920
back ticks. You don't need to remember this because we'll be doing plenty of it. Just gives you

07:32.920 --> 07:39.240
a sense of how this works. And one final tip people tend to say for these things, try and focus

07:39.240 --> 08:09.839
on the positives, try and focus on things that it should do. Don't do too many negatives like like I have there never Python 3. You can use it from time to time but not too many because allalands aren't so good at handling negatives, strangely, that they're not so coherent at remembering things that and try not to have too much of like do not over-complicate that I got in here.

08:09.839 --> 08:14.240
But you don't want too much of that because they tend to be less coherent with it. Focus on what

08:14.240 --> 08:20.000
it should do, less of what it should not do. And so, a final point that I'll make here is that

08:20.000 --> 08:34.460
there was a school of thought last year, and the school of thought very much was, look, your was look your success with agent coding is from spending time with a really

08:34.460 --> 08:40.519
really excellent agents.md that is where you need to sweat you need to put

08:40.519 --> 08:45.799
time energy effort into agents.md you need to have one at the top you need to

08:45.799 --> 08:49.240
have one in different sub directories you need to work on making it really

08:49.240 --> 08:54.080
really good and have other documents that support it. You need to

08:54.080 --> 09:02.240
continually supplement with extra files, extra planning files, success criteria, steps that need

09:02.240 --> 09:10.320
to be taken to test. You need to continually rewrite your agents.md again and again pruning it,

09:10.320 --> 09:17.399
removing things that don't matter anymore, adding in new things. And indeed, you can have your LLM rewrite it for you,

09:17.399 --> 09:19.279
but be giving it very clear instructions

09:19.279 --> 09:20.759
because it is precious.

09:20.759 --> 09:24.519
And every line counts and LLM's love to add

09:24.519 --> 09:27.440
to agents.md and you need to make sure that it's tight.

09:27.440 --> 09:32.440
And often stop your AI agent, rewrite, prune,

09:32.799 --> 09:40.960
and then reset and store your agent again fresh context completely clear and new agents.md

09:40.960 --> 09:51.200
That is it's hard work but that certainly as of last year was the prevailing way to get the most value out of AI agents.

09:51.200 --> 10:03.519
And you can probably tell there is a new way, a new school of thought, and the 2026 mindset. It's a kind of let it hang out.

10:04.159 --> 10:11.840
Be more comfortable giving up the reins, let it go. You focus on the end goal, focus on you what

10:11.840 --> 10:21.759
you want the LLM to achieve at the end of it. Use some of the new features that we will cover over the next few weeks, skills, having more loops,

10:21.759 --> 10:28.159
using agents, sub-agents and having a swarm of different agents, let it have the end goal,

10:28.159 --> 10:35.919
let itself correct and just let it be, let it do its thing. That is the kind of prevailing

10:35.919 --> 10:42.200
2026 mindset, which is quite a departure from the 2025 mindset.

10:42.200 --> 10:44.120
And I get, I hate to break it to you.

10:44.120 --> 10:48.039
I hate to break it to you, but I am still

10:48.039 --> 10:50.600
of the 2025 School of Thought.

10:50.600 --> 10:52.240
I think it's more nuanced than this.

